def safe_clone_dict(data: dict) -> dict:
    def _clone(obj, seen: set):
        if isinstance(obj, dict):
            if id(obj) in seen:
                return '[CIRCULAR_REF]'
            seen.add(id(obj))
            result = {k: _clone(v, seen) for k, v in obj.items()}
            seen.remove(id(obj))
            return result
        elif isinstance(obj, list):
            if id(obj) in seen:
                return '[CIRCULAR_REF]'
            seen.add(id(obj))
            result = [_clone(item, seen) for item in obj]
            seen.remove(id(obj))
            return result
        else:
            return obj

    return _clone(data, set())
```

The `seen` set tracks `id()`s of containers currently on the recursion path. When a container is encountered that's already being processed (i.e., it's an ancestor in the traversal), it's a cycle — we return the marker string instead of recursing. Removing the id after processing (backtracking) ensures that shared-but-not-circular references (e.g., the same list appearing under two different keys) are cloned independently rather than being falsely flagged.
